note When the graph's camera moves, and why it otherwise does not. Pan and zoom are two plain fields on the graph: dragging the background and the wheel write them directly, and nothing else is allowed to, so a rebuild, an edit or a re-index never slides the view out from under the reader. Two things ask for a move: a new focus (centre it, or align its top when it is taller than the canvas) and the fit button (the largest font at which the whole tree fits, then pan to its top-left). A node button that changes the tree's shape (callees, callers, less) instead keeps its node still: the pan follows the relayout so the button stays under the pointer and the new nodes appear beside it. The layout is in cells and rows, which no zoom changes, so fit settles both zoom and pan in one frame instead of converging over several.

